=== layout: section

# Chapter 18: Cloning and Stem Cells

=== layout: subsection

# Topic 25: Characteristics of Stem Cells

=== layout: one-panel

# 25. Questions: What Makes A Stem Cell?

@body

- State the two core properties: self-renewal and differentiation potential.
- Compare totipotent, pluripotent, multipotent, oligopotent, and unipotent cells. Give an example of each.
- Where do adult stem cells work in the body? How can pluripotent cells be made without using embryos?
- How would you test whether a culture actually behaves like a stem-cell population?

=== layout: two-panels

# 25. Two Jobs, Not One Label

@left

- *Self-renewal:* a stem cell makes at least one daughter that remains a stem cell.
- *Differentiation:* it can make more specialized descendants under the right conditions.
- A marker protein alone is not proof. Colony growth, repeated passaging, and a directed differentiation test ask what the cells can do.

@right

![Self-renewal, proliferation, and differentiation](assets/set5_stem_cells/stem_cell_properties.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 25. Potency Is A Developmental Range

@left

- *Totipotent:* embryo plus supporting extraembryonic tissues.
- *Pluripotent:* many body-cell types from all three germ layers.
- *Multipotent:* related tissue lineages, such as blood cells.
- *Oligopotent / unipotent:* a few / one closely related fate.

@right

## Source Is Not Potency

Established embryonic stem-cell lines are pluripotent. Totipotency belongs to the zygote and early blastomeres.

Use the historical diagram on the next slide to identify why these categories should be kept separate.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![25. Read This Potency Diagram Critically](assets/set5_stem_cells/stem_cell_potency_hierarchy.png)

@caption

Topic 25: Historical Classification: Embryonic Stem-Cell Lines Are Pluripotent, Not Totipotent.

=== layout: two-panels

# 25. Symmetric Or Asymmetric Division?

@left

- *Symmetric self-renewal:* one stem cell makes two stem cells. Useful when a tissue must expand its reserve.
- *Asymmetric division:* one daughter retains the stem-cell state while the other begins differentiation.
- The balance controls tissue maintenance. Too little renewal depletes a tissue; too much can expand an abnormal clone.

@right

![Symmetric and asymmetric stem-cell division](assets/set5_stem_cells/stem_division_crop.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 25. The Niche Helps Set Cell Fate

@left

- A *niche* supplies neighboring cells, matrix, nutrients, and signals.
- Wnt often supports renewal; Notch can maintain state; BMP effects depend on tissue and context.
- *Quiescence* is a reversible resting state that protects a long-lived pool.
- Bone marrow, intestinal crypts, and skin show that stem cells do not act alone.

@right

![Stem-cell properties in a tissue context](assets/set5_stem_cells/stem_cell_properties.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 25. Different Sources, Different Potency

@left

- Embryonic stem cells: pluripotent cells from an early embryo.
- Adult stem cells: usually multipotent cells that maintain a tissue.
- iPSCs: differentiated cells reset toward pluripotency by reprogramming factors.
- Every line still needs identity, function, and safety evidence.

@right

![Embryonic stem-cell culture from an early embryo](assets/set5_stem_cells/embryonic_stem_cell_culture_2023.png)

@citation

Takahashi and Yamanaka, 2006: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2006.07.024

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Embryonic stem-cell culture from an early embryo](assets/set5_stem_cells/embryonic_stem_cell_culture_2023.png)

@caption

Topic 25: From the Inner Cell Mass to an Embryonic Stem-Cell Culture


=== layout: subsection

# Topic 26: Genomic Stability In iPSCs

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. Questions: Genome Quality

@body

- What is an iPSC, and how does it differ from an embryonic stem cell?
- What can a karyotype reveal? What extra information comes from copy-number and sequence analysis?
- Why can donor age, reprogramming, and long culture select cells with genetic changes?
- Which routine practices reduce risk before a line is used for research or therapy?

=== layout: two-panels

# 26. Reprogramming and Quality

@left

- Reprogramming changes gene activity, not every DNA change.
- A *karyotype* reveals large chromosome changes.
- Copy-number assays and sequencing find smaller changes.
- A rare faster-growing cell can take over a culture.

@right

{table-style=comparison}
| Assay | What It Can Reveal |
| --- | --- |
| Karyotype | Large chromosome changes |
| Copy-number analysis | Gains and losses |
| Sequencing | Smaller DNA variants |

=== layout: two-panels

# 26. A Useful Release Checklist

@left

- *Identity:* records and cell-bank traceability.
- *Genome:* karyotype plus copy-number or sequence assay.
- *State:* marker pattern and directed differentiation.
- *Control:* sterility, mycoplasma checks, passage history, early banked clones.

The result applies to a bank and passage range, not all future cultures.

@right

## A Defined Release Window

1. Identify the bank.
2. Specify the passage range.
3. Check genome and cell state.
4. Check contamination and function.
5. Reassess after further culture.

=== layout: two-panels

# 26. Selection During Culture

@left

- Culture rewards faster survival or division, not therapeutic benefit.
- Chromosome changes can let one subclone outcompete the intended population.
- Limit passages, bank early clones, and compare later cultures.
- The product needs both its intended identity and controlled behavior.

@right

{table-style=comparison}
| Culture Can Select | Therapy Still Requires |
| --- | --- |
| Faster growth | Intended cell function |
| Better survival in a dish | Controlled behavior in tissue |
| A dominant subclone | Genome checks |

@citation

Laurent et al., 2011: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.stem.2010.12.003

=== layout: subsection

# Topic 27: iPSC-Derived Cell Therapies

=== layout: one-panel

# 27. Questions: iPSC Products

@body

- Choose an eye, heart, or Parkinson's disease example. Which cell type is the intended product, and what function should it restore or support?
- Trace the workflow: make iPSCs, guide differentiation, check identity and function, then deliver cells.
- How can a team look for residual undifferentiated cells, inappropriate growth, and immune rejection?
- Compare an autologous product with an allogeneic product. Where does HLA matching matter?

=== layout: two-panels

# 27. The Product Is The Differentiated Cell

@left

1. Start with a characterized iPSC bank.
2. Apply timed signals to direct a chosen lineage.
3. Enrich and test the intended cells.
4. Deliver them where they can survive and function.

The iPSC is an expandable starting material. The clinical product is the selected, tested descendant population.

@right

{table-style=comparison}
| Question | Evidence Needed |
| --- | --- |
| Identity | Intended cell markers |
| Function | Relevant activity assay |
| Safety | Controlled growth and purity |
| Benefit | Disease-relevant outcome |

=== layout: one-panel

# 27. Identity, Function, and Safety

@body

- *Identity:* morphology and markers support the cell type.
- *Function:* retinal, heart, or neural cells need relevant assays.
- *Safety:* look for residual pluripotent cells and excess growth.
- *Delivery:* cells must survive at the site without harmful immunity.

=== layout: one-panel

# 27. Autologous Or Allogeneic?

@body

- *Autologous:* closer genetic match; slow individualized manufacture.
- *Allogeneic:* standardized batches; HLA matching and rejection can matter.
- Neither label proves better clinical performance.
- The disease, target tissue, manufacturing plan, and outcome decide credibility.

=== layout: two-panels

# 27. What Counts As An Outcome?

@left

- Use a disease-relevant outcome, not only a photograph of implanted cells.
- Eye example: retinal imaging, graft survival, and prespecified visual function.
- Heart example: function plus arrhythmia and abnormal-growth monitoring.
- Early studies test safety and feasibility before durable efficacy.

@right

{table-style=comparison}
| Observation | Still Needs Testing |
| --- | --- |
| Cells remain at the site | Do they function? |
| A marker is expressed | Is the mechanism restored? |
| A symptom improves | Does the benefit persist? |

@citation

Mandai et al., 2017: https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1608368

=== layout: subsection

# Topic 28: Stem Cell-Derived Immune Cells

=== layout: one-panel

# 28. Questions: Immune-Cell Therapy

@body

- Which immune-cell types can be made from pluripotent stem cells: NK cells, macrophages, or T cells? What does each normally do?
- How would you test activity: target-cell killing for NK or T cells, or particle uptake for macrophages?
- What does a CAR add to an immune cell? What can it not guarantee?
- What safety signals and persistence questions matter after infusion?

=== layout: two-panels

# 28. Match the Cell to the Assay

@left

- *NK cells* can kill stressed or antibody-tagged cells.
- *T cells* can recognize antigens and can be engineered with a CAR.
- *Macrophages* engulf material and shape inflammation.
- Killing, cytokine, and off-target assays answer different questions.

@right

![iPSC-derived immune-cell manufacturing sequence](assets/set5_stem_cells/immune_manufacturing_2025.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 28. Manufacturing Is A Chain Of Evidence

@left

1. Differentiate a banked pluripotent line toward one immune lineage.
2. Expand the population under controlled conditions.
3. Confirm identity and test a relevant function.
4. Test dose, persistence, unintended activity, and product consistency.

@right

{table-style=comparison}
| Cell Product | Example Functional Test |
| --- | --- |
| NK cell | Target-cell killing |
| T cell | Response to antigen |
| Macrophage | Phagocytosis |

=== layout: two-panels

# 28. CARs Redirect Recognition

@left

- A CAR links target recognition to activation in an immune cell.
- The target must be enriched on tumor cells; normal cells can also be attacked.
- Overactivation can cause cytokine-release symptoms; persistence can help or harm.
- Topic 31 returns to CAR-T and CAR-NK in the cancer chapter.

@right

![A T cell before and after addition of a chimeric antigen receptor](assets/set5_stem_cells/car_t_cell_2025.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 28. Early-Study Measurements

@left

- Product identity, viability, dose, and lot consistency.
- In-body persistence and trafficking to the intended tissue.
- Safety: infusion reactions, unintended injury, and infections.
- A disease-relevant activity measure, not one dramatic patient story.

@right

![Manufacturing prompt for a defined immune-cell product](assets/set5_stem_cells/immune_manufacturing_2025.png)

=== layout: subsection

# Topic 29: Animal Cloning

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. Questions: What Does SCNT Copy?

@body

- Trace somatic cell nuclear transfer (SCNT): donor cell, enucleated egg, activation, early embryo, surrogate.
- Why was Dolly significant? What do the numbers say about efficiency?
- Why can incomplete reprogramming and imprinting errors disrupt development?
- Which applications raise animal-welfare, genetic-diversity, and commercialization questions?

=== layout: two-panels

# 29. Somatic Cell Nuclear Transfer

@left

1. Remove the nucleus from an unfertilized egg.
2. Place a donor somatic-cell nucleus into that cytoplasm.
3. Activate the reconstructed egg so that it begins early development.
4. Transfer a suitable embryo to a synchronized surrogate.

The clone receives nuclear DNA from the donor cell, but it does not receive a copied developmental history or the donor's egg cytoplasm.

@right

![SCNT steps used in the Dolly experiment](assets/set5_stem_cells/dolly_annual_2025.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 29. Dolly: Evidence and Efficiency

@left

- Born in 1996; announced with the 1997 paper.
- First mammal cloned from an *adult somatic cell*, not the first cloned mammal.
- 277 reconstructed embryos produced 29 suitable for transfer; one pregnancy produced Dolly.
- Egg cytoplasm reprogrammed an adult-cell nucleus.

@right

![The donor, egg, surrogate, and clone](assets/set5_stem_cells/dolly_legacy.png)

@citation

Wilmut et al., 1997: https://doi.org/10.1038/385810a0

=== layout: big-image

@image

![29. Following the Nucleus Through SCNT](assets/set5_stem_cells/dolly_legacy.png)

@caption

Topic 29: Following the Nucleus Through SCNT

=== layout: two-panels

# 29. Low Efficiency Has Biological Meaning

@left

- SCNT asks egg cytoplasm to reset a differentiated nucleus for embryonic development.
- Many embryos fail before implantation or during pregnancy.
- Incomplete resetting and imprinting errors can disrupt placental or fetal development.
- Do not use Dolly's history as a universal premature-aging claim.

@right

![Dolly as a case study in SCNT efficiency](assets/set5_stem_cells/dolly_annual_2025.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 29. Why Clone Animals?

@left

- Research models can standardize a genotype for a focused experiment.
- Livestock and performance-animal programs may try to preserve the genetics of an unusual animal.
- Conservation proposals must still protect habitat, population size, and genetic diversity.
- Animal welfare is central because low efficiency means many embryos, pregnancies, and surrogate animals can be involved.

@right

![Questions about animal welfare and cloning applications](assets/set5_stem_cells/cloning_ethics_2025.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 29. Clones Of Polo Horses

@left

- What is preserved when a champion horse is cloned?
- Nuclear genotype is only one contributor to athletic performance.
- Training, development, microbiome, epigenetics, and environment are not copied as a finished product.
- A clone is not a guaranteed duplicate career.

@right

![Cloned polo horses](assets/set5_stem_cells/polo_video_2025.png)

@citation

Cloning polo horses: https://www.wired.com/story/cloning-came-to-polo-things-got-uncivilized/

=== layout: two-panels

# 29. Pet Cloning and Individuality

@left

- Commercial pet cloning preserves viable cells, then uses SCNT.
- A clone shares much of a donor's nuclear genotype but has its own environment and experiences.
- Ask about success rate, animal welfare, cost, and what outcome is actually promised.
- The course video is for discussion, not an endorsement.

@right

![commercial pet cloning](assets/set5_stem_cells/pet_cloning_legacy.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. Pet-Cloning Resources

@body

- Compare the company's explanation with the biological constraints of SCNT.
- Company information: https://viagenpets.com/dog-cloning/
- Watch the video: https://youtu.be/8WM5ZH_8x2w
- What evidence would distinguish shared nuclear DNA from a promise of the same appearance or behavior?
